From 40208ee76cd3fd38b509dd1e2b1753fb1fb830bb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kayori <thatzokay@proton.me>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 15:34:46 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] grate: unroll bounded shader loops

Set the NIR unroll budget to 32 iterations. Grate already advertises
zero control flow depth, so NIR flattens branches to selections after
unrolling. Leave runtime and otherwise unbounded loops unsupported.

NVIDIA's T30 compiler emits straight-line programs for the two- and
four-iteration fixtures, including a conditional break, and rejects
the runtime-count loop. Mesa now follows the same strategy. Both
bounded loops pass the CPU model at 200x150 and 1024x768 within 1/255
away from nearest-sampling boundaries; the previous driver rejects
them. Branch comparison precision is fixed separately.
---
 src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c b/src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c
index 8db36ad91dc..1fadfb7d8dd 100644
--- a/src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c
+++ b/src/gallium/drivers/grate/grate_screen.c
@@ -478,6 +478,7 @@ static const nir_shader_compiler_options grate_base_compiler_options = {
    .force_indirect_unrolling_sampler = true,
    .max_unroll_iterations = 32,
     */
+   .max_unroll_iterations = 32,
    .lower_fdiv = true,
    .no_integers = true,
 };
